1
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:44,190
The little lord's been dreaming again.

2
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:54,480
- We have visitors. - I don't want to see anyone.

3
00:02:54,480 --> 00:02:57,320
Really? If I was cooped up all day

4
00:02:57,350 --> 00:03:00,140
with no one but this old bat for company, I'd go mad.

5
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:04,160
Anyway, you don't have a choice. Robb's waiting.

6
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:07,250
- I don't want to go. - Neither do I.

7
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:08,950
But Robb's Lord of Winterfell,

8
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:12,070
Which means I do what he says, and you do what I say.

9
00:03:13,010 --> 00:03:14,630
Hodor!

10
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:21,950
- Hodor? - Help Bran down the hall.

11
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:23,390
Hodor.

12
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:33,680
I must say I received a slightly warmer welcome on my last visit.

13
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:36,280
Any man of the night's watch is welcome at Winterfell.

14
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:38,830
Any man of the night's watch, but not I, eh, boy?

15
00:03:38,830 --> 00:03:41,160
I'm not your boy, Lannister.

16
00:03:41,160 --> 00:03:43,300
I'm Lord of Winterfell while my father is away.

17
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:45,890
Then you might learn a lord's courtesy.

18
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:51,130
So it's true.

19
00:03:52,570 --> 00:03:54,140
Hello, Bran.

20
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,120
Do you remember anything about what happened?

21
00:03:57,120 --> 00:04:00,050
He has no memory of that day.

22
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:02,790
- Curious. - Why are you here?

23
00:04:03,750 --> 00:04:06,430
Would your charming companion be so kind as to kneel?

24
00:04:06,430 --> 00:04:09,950
- My neck is beginning to hurt. - Kneel, Hodor.

25
00:04:13,060 --> 00:04:16,150
- Do you like to ride, Bran? - Yes.

26
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:18,180
Well, I mean I did like to.

27
00:04:18,220 --> 00:04:20,450
- The boy has lost the use of his legs. - What of it?

28
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:22,630
With the right horse and saddle, even a cripple can ride.

29
00:04:22,630 --> 00:04:25,720
- I'm not a cripple. - Then I'm not a dwarf.

30
00:04:25,720 --> 00:04:27,880
My father will rejoice to hear it.

31
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:29,510
I have a gift for you.

32
00:04:30,250 --> 00:04:33,400
Give that to your saddler. He'll provide the rest.

33
00:04:34,550 --> 00:04:37,160
You must shape the horse to the rider.

34
00:04:37,270 --> 00:04:39,780
Start with a yearling and teach it to respond to the reins

35
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:41,570
and to the boy's voice.

36
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:44,680
Will I really be able to ride?

37
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:45,960
You will.

38
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:48,620
On horseback you will be as tall as any of them.

39
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:51,560
Is this some kind of trick? Why do you want to help him?

40
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:53,230
I have a tender spot in my heart

41
00:04:53,230 --> 00:04:56,210
for cripples, bastards and broken things.

42
00:04:57,660 --> 00:04:59,830
You've done my brother a kindness.

43
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:01,960
The hospitality of Winterfell is yours.

44
00:05:01,960 --> 00:05:05,390
Spare me your false courtesies, Lord Stark.

45
00:05:05,390 --> 00:05:07,220
There's a brothel outside your walls.

46
00:05:07,250 --> 00:05:10,760
There I'll find a bed and both of us can sleep easier.

47
00:05:15,250 --> 00:05:17,310
Couldn't resist some northern ass?

48
00:05:17,850 --> 00:05:20,620
If you like redheads, ask for Ros.

49
00:05:20,650 --> 00:05:23,520
Come to see me off, Greyjoy? Kind of you.

50
00:05:23,550 --> 00:05:25,780
Your master doesn't seem to like Lannisters.

51
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:29,310
- He's not my master. - No, of course not.

52
00:05:29,990 --> 00:05:31,080
What happened here?

53
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:33,480
Where is lady Stark? Why didn't she receive me?

54
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:34,660
She wasn't feeling well.

55
00:05:34,660 --> 00:05:37,000
She's not in Winterfell, is she? Where did she go?

56
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,470
- My lady's whereabouts... - My lady?

57
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:43,850
Your loyalty to your captors is touching.

58
00:05:43,850 --> 00:05:46,910
Tell me, how do you think Balon Greyjoy would feel

59
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:50,220
if he could see his only surviving son has turned lackey?

60
00:05:51,120 --> 00:05:54,510
I still remember seeing my father's fleet burn in Lannisport.

61
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:56,880
I believe your uncles were responsible?

62
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:58,380
Must have been a pretty sight.

63
00:05:58,380 --> 00:06:01,200
Nothing prettier than watching sailors burn alive.

64
00:06:01,960 --> 00:06:04,760
Yes, a great victory for your people.

65
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:07,880
- Shame how it all turned out. - We were outnumbered 10 to one...

66
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:09,430
A stupid rebellion then.

67
00:06:10,110 --> 00:06:11,780
I suppose your father realized that

68
00:06:11,780 --> 00:06:13,900
when your brothers died in battle.

69
00:06:14,260 --> 00:06:17,850
Now here you are, your enemy's squire.

70
00:06:17,850 --> 00:06:20,310
- Careful, Imp. - I've offended you.

71
00:06:20,530 --> 00:06:23,520
Forgive me, it's been a rough morning.

72
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:26,660
Anyway, don't despair.

73
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:28,480
I'm a constant disappointment to my own father.

74
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:30,150
And I've learned to live with it.

75
00:06:31,190 --> 00:06:34,180
Your next tumble with Ros is on me.

76
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:36,940
I'll try not to wear her out.

77
00:06:41,530 --> 00:06:44,190
Shoulder, legs.

78
00:06:48,510 --> 00:06:52,300
Leg, shoulder, leg.

79
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:55,370
Left foot forward. Good.

80
00:06:55,370 --> 00:06:57,340
Now pivot as you deliver the stroke.

81
00:06:57,340 --> 00:06:59,060
Put all your weight behind it.

82
00:06:59,350 --> 00:07:01,720
What in seven hells is that?

83
00:07:02,570 --> 00:07:04,790
They'll need an eighth hell to fit him in.

84
00:07:06,550 --> 00:07:08,140
Tell them your name.

85
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:11,950
Samwell Tarly, Of Horn Hill...

86
00:07:11,950 --> 00:07:13,670
I mean, I was of Horn Hill.

87
00:07:15,030 --> 00:07:18,170
- I've come to take the black. - Come to take the black pudding.

88
00:07:18,870 --> 00:07:21,310
Well, you couldn't be any worse than you look.

89
00:07:21,970 --> 00:07:23,390
Rast...

90
00:07:24,110 --> 00:07:25,710
See what he can do.

91
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:36,080
I yield.

92
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:38,510
- Please, no more. - On your feet.

93
00:07:38,510 --> 00:07:40,200
Pick up your sword.

94
00:07:40,820 --> 00:07:44,100
Hit him till he finds his feet.

95
00:07:53,020 --> 00:07:55,420
It seems they've run short of poachers and thieves down south.

96
00:07:55,450 --> 00:07:58,020
Now they send us squealing bloody pigs.

97
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:00,250
- Jon! - Again, harder.

98
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:04,230
- I yield! - Enough!

99
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:06,490
He yielded.

100
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:11,420
Looks like the bastard's in love.

101
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:15,980
All right then, Lord Snow,

102
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:17,860
you wish to defend your lady love,

103
00:08:17,860 --> 00:08:19,280
let's make it an exercise.

104
00:08:19,320 --> 00:08:24,180
You two. Three of you ought to be sufficient to make Lady Piggy squeal.

105
00:08:24,290 --> 00:08:27,800
All you've got to do is get past the bastard.

106
00:08:30,040 --> 00:08:32,690
- Are you sure you want to do this? - No.

107
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:48,930
Yield yield yield! I yield.

108
00:08:51,250 --> 00:08:53,020
We're done for today.

109
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:54,720
Go clean the armory.

110
00:08:54,750 --> 00:08:56,870
That's all you're good for.

111
00:09:00,420 --> 00:09:03,150
- Well fought! - Piss off.

112
00:09:07,340 --> 00:09:08,960
Did he hurt you?

113
00:09:09,170 --> 00:09:10,740
I've had worse.

114
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:15,140
You can call me Sam... if you want.

115
00:09:15,740 --> 00:09:17,040
My mother calls me Sam...

116
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:19,090
It's not going to get any easier, you know?

117
00:09:19,090 --> 00:09:20,780
You're going to have to defend yourself.

118
00:09:20,780 --> 00:09:22,870
Why didn't you get up and fight?

119
00:09:23,670 --> 00:09:25,450
I wanted to.

120
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:28,490
- I just couldn't. - Why not?

121
00:09:30,540 --> 00:09:32,040
I'm a coward.

122
00:09:33,710 --> 00:09:34,850
My father always says so.

123
00:09:34,850 --> 00:09:36,630
The wall's no place for cowards.

124
00:09:36,630 --> 00:09:38,780
You're right. I'm sorry.

125
00:09:39,050 --> 00:09:42,090
I just... wanted to thank you.

126
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:54,760
A bloody coward.

127
00:09:54,920 --> 00:09:57,180
People saw us talking to him.

128
00:09:57,180 --> 00:09:59,180
Now they'll think we're cowards too.

129
00:09:59,220 --> 00:10:00,430
You're too stupid to be a coward.

130
00:10:00,430 --> 00:10:02,630
- You're too stupid to be a... - Quick now,

131
00:10:02,830 --> 00:10:04,620
before summer's over.

132
00:10:06,770 --> 00:10:08,140
C'mere!

133
00:10:42,620 --> 00:10:46,510
Vaes Dothrak... the city of the horselords.

134
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:49,670
A pile of mud.

135
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:51,650
Mud and shit and twigs...

136
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:53,440
best these savages can do.

137
00:10:53,440 --> 00:10:55,190
These are my people now.

138
00:10:55,340 --> 00:10:56,980
You shouldn't call them savages.

139
00:10:56,980 --> 00:10:59,620
I'll call them what I like, because they're my people.

140
00:10:59,620 --> 00:11:01,340
This is my army.

141
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:05,110
Khal Drogo is marching the wrong way with my army.

142
00:11:24,280 --> 00:11:27,580
If my brother was given an army of Dothraki,

143
00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:30,740
could you conquer the Seven Kingdoms?

144
00:11:31,380 --> 00:11:34,250
The Dothraki have never crossed the narrow sea.

145
00:11:34,250 --> 00:11:36,610
They fear any water their horses can't drink.

146
00:11:36,610 --> 00:11:38,210
But if they did?

147
00:11:39,670 --> 00:11:42,780
King Robert is fool enough to meet them in open battle,

148
00:11:43,010 --> 00:11:45,580
but the men advising him are different.

149
00:11:45,620 --> 00:11:47,360
And you know these men?

150
00:11:47,360 --> 00:11:49,310
I fought beside them once,

151
00:11:49,880 --> 00:11:51,770
long ago.

152
00:11:52,050 --> 00:11:54,630
Now Ned Stark wants my head.

153
00:11:54,840 --> 00:11:57,110
He drove me from my land.

154
00:11:58,450 --> 00:12:00,690
You sold slaves.

155
00:12:01,750 --> 00:12:03,260
Aye.

156
00:12:05,020 --> 00:12:08,110
- Why? - I had no money

157
00:12:08,110 --> 00:12:10,420
and an expensive wife.

158
00:12:10,620 --> 00:12:11,900
And where is she now?

159
00:12:12,100 --> 00:12:13,980
In another place,

160
00:12:14,450 --> 00:12:16,360
with another man.

161
00:12:23,160 --> 00:12:25,570
- Your Grace? - Yes, my dear?

162
00:12:25,790 --> 00:12:28,080
They call you the last dragon...

163
00:12:28,120 --> 00:12:29,770
They do.

164
00:12:32,080 --> 00:12:35,430
You have dragon's blood in your veins?

165
00:12:36,220 --> 00:12:38,480
It's entirely possible.

166
00:12:40,270 --> 00:12:41,850
What happened to the dragons?

167
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:44,820
I was told that brave men killed them all.

168
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:51,250
The brave men didn't kill dragons.

169
00:12:52,030 --> 00:12:54,230
The brave men rode them.

170
00:12:54,620 --> 00:12:56,700
Rode them from Valyria to build

171
00:12:56,700 --> 00:13:00,450
the greatest civilization this world has ever seen.

172
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:05,140
The breath of the greatest dragon forged the Iron Throne,

173
00:13:05,270 --> 00:13:08,130
which the usurper is keeping warm for me.

174
00:13:09,250 --> 00:13:11,780
The swords of the vanquished,

175
00:13:12,490 --> 00:13:14,550
a thousand of them...

176
00:13:14,550 --> 00:13:19,440
melted together like so many candles.

177
00:13:22,350 --> 00:13:25,660
I have always wanted to see a dragon.

178
00:13:27,080 --> 00:13:29,910
There is nothing in the world that I would rather see.

179
00:13:29,910 --> 00:13:32,630
Really. Why dragons?

180
00:13:33,220 --> 00:13:34,920
They can fly.

181
00:13:34,950 --> 00:13:38,050
And wherever they are, just a few flaps of their wings

182
00:13:38,080 --> 00:13:40,170
and they're somewhere else...

183
00:13:40,720 --> 00:13:42,310
far away.

184
00:13:45,350 --> 00:13:47,330
And they can kill.

185
00:13:47,720 --> 00:13:52,560
Anyone or anything that tries to hurt them

186
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:56,730
gets burned away to nothing...

187
00:13:56,730 --> 00:14:00,940
melted...like so many candles.

188
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:09,020
Yes. Seeing a dragon would make me very happy.

189
00:14:09,050 --> 00:14:11,180
Well, after 15 years in a pleasure house,

190
00:14:11,180 --> 00:14:13,850
I imagine just seeing the sky makes you happy.

191
00:14:13,880 --> 00:14:16,950
I was not locked in. I have seen things.

192
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:18,390
- What have you seen? - I've seen...

193
00:14:18,390 --> 00:14:22,780
a man from Asshai with a dagger of real dragonglass.

194
00:14:22,820 --> 00:14:25,780
I've seen a man who could change his face

195
00:14:25,790 --> 00:14:29,130
the way that other men change their clothes.

196
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:33,630
And I've seen a pirate who wore his weight in gold

197
00:14:33,630 --> 00:14:38,020
and whose ship had sails of colored silk.

198
00:14:38,820 --> 00:14:40,770
So...

199
00:14:42,080 --> 00:14:44,100
Have you seen one?

200
00:14:44,580 --> 00:14:48,020
- A pirate ship? - A dragon.

201
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:50,020
No.

202
00:14:51,110 --> 00:14:54,220
No, the last one died many years before I was born.

203
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:57,180
I'll tell you what I have seen:

204
00:14:58,490 --> 00:15:00,500
Their skulls.

205
00:15:00,850 --> 00:15:03,740
They used to decorate the throne room in the Red Keep.

206
00:15:03,930 --> 00:15:05,590
When I was very young, just three or four,

207
00:15:05,590 --> 00:15:07,980
my father used to walk me down the rows

208
00:15:07,980 --> 00:15:10,260
and I'd recite their names for him.

209
00:15:10,470 --> 00:15:13,040
When I got them all right, he'd give me a sweet.

210
00:15:14,360 --> 00:15:15,590
The ones closest to the door

211
00:15:15,590 --> 00:15:17,410
were the last ones they were able to hatch

212
00:15:17,410 --> 00:15:18,900
and they were all stunted and wrong.

213
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:22,400
Skulls no bigger than dog skulls.

214
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:27,930
But as you got closer to the Iron Throne...

215
00:15:28,780 --> 00:15:30,880
They got bigger

216
00:15:31,080 --> 00:15:34,730
and bigger and bigger.

217
00:15:36,950 --> 00:15:38,900
There was Ghiscar

218
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:42,110
And Valryon,

219
00:15:42,110 --> 00:15:43,850
Vermithrax,

220
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:45,970
Essovius...

221
00:15:45,970 --> 00:15:47,880
Archonei,

222
00:15:48,890 --> 00:15:52,040
Meraxes,

223
00:15:53,270 --> 00:15:56,140
Vhagar...

224
00:15:56,850 --> 00:15:59,280
and Balerion the Dread...

225
00:15:59,720 --> 00:16:02,280
...whose fire forged

226
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:04,960
the Seven Kingdoms

227
00:16:04,960 --> 00:16:06,700
into one.

228
00:16:11,150 --> 00:16:13,380
What happened to the skulls?

229
00:16:15,970 --> 00:16:17,750
I don't know.

230
00:16:17,780 --> 00:16:21,300
The Usurper had them smashed to powder, I expect.

231
00:16:21,950 --> 00:16:23,630
Scattered to the wind.

232
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:29,110
That's very sad.

233
00:16:29,950 --> 00:16:31,530
Yes, it is.

234
00:16:33,420 --> 00:16:35,150
What did I buy you for?

235
00:16:35,350 --> 00:16:38,480
- To make me sad? - No, your Grace.

236
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:43,150
To, uh, teach your sister.

237
00:16:43,180 --> 00:16:45,720
To teach my sister how to be a better lover?

238
00:16:46,130 --> 00:16:49,480
You think I bought you to make Khal Drogo happy?

239
00:16:53,950 --> 00:16:56,080
Oh, you pretty little idiot.

240
00:16:58,980 --> 00:17:00,700
Go on then. Get on with it.

241
00:17:11,020 --> 00:17:13,960
Someday your husband will sit there

242
00:17:13,960 --> 00:17:16,410
and you will sit by his side.

243
00:17:16,620 --> 00:17:18,340
and one day, before too long,

244
00:17:18,340 --> 00:17:21,360
you will present your son to the court.

245
00:17:21,560 --> 00:17:23,720
All the lords of Westeros

246
00:17:23,750 --> 00:17:26,040
will gather here to see the little prince...

247
00:17:26,040 --> 00:17:27,830
What if I have a girl?

248
00:17:27,830 --> 00:17:31,640
Gods be good, you'll have boys and girls and plenty of them.

249
00:17:31,640 --> 00:17:33,920
What if I only have girls?

250
00:17:33,950 --> 00:17:35,760
I wouldn't worry about that.

251
00:17:36,120 --> 00:17:38,940
Jeyne poole's mother had five children... all of them girls.

252
00:17:38,940 --> 00:17:41,350
Yes, but it's highly unlikely.

253
00:17:41,380 --> 00:17:43,020
But what if?

254
00:17:43,790 --> 00:17:47,120
Well, if you only had girls,

255
00:17:47,150 --> 00:17:48,670
I suppose the throne would pass

256
00:17:48,670 --> 00:17:51,040
to prince Joffrey's little brother.

257
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:53,630
And everyone would hate me.

258
00:17:54,220 --> 00:17:55,990
Nobody could ever hate you.

259
00:17:56,170 --> 00:17:58,330
- Joffrey does. - Nonsense.

260
00:17:58,730 --> 00:18:01,120
Why would you say such a thing?

261
00:18:02,880 --> 00:18:05,520
That business with the wolves? Sansa,

262
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:07,050
I've told you a hundred times:

263
00:18:07,050 --> 00:18:10,230
- a direwolf is not... - Please shut up about it.

264
00:18:11,620 --> 00:18:13,690
Do you remember your lessons?

265
00:18:15,770 --> 00:18:18,420
Who built the Iron Throne?

266
00:18:18,420 --> 00:18:20,120
Aegon the Conqueror.

267
00:18:20,150 --> 00:18:22,210
And who built the Red Keep?

268
00:18:22,210 --> 00:18:23,780
Maegor the Cruel.

269
00:18:23,820 --> 00:18:25,680
And how many years did it take to build...

270
00:18:25,720 --> 00:18:28,660
My grandfather and uncle were murdered here, weren't they?

271
00:18:30,110 --> 00:18:33,970
They were killed on the orders of King Aerys, yes.

272
00:18:33,970 --> 00:18:38,270
- The mad king. - Commonly known as The Mad king.

273
00:18:38,470 --> 00:18:40,630
Why were they killed?

274
00:18:41,370 --> 00:18:44,140
You should be speak to your father about these matters.

275
00:18:44,330 --> 00:18:46,620
I don't want to speak to my father... ever.

276
00:18:46,650 --> 00:18:49,800
Sansa, you will find it in your heart

277
00:18:49,800 --> 00:18:51,650
to forgive your father.

278
00:18:52,460 --> 00:18:53,690
No, I won't.

279
00:18:54,240 --> 00:18:56,900
It's the Hand's tournament that's causing all this trouble, my lords.

280
00:18:56,900 --> 00:18:58,890
The King's tournament.

281
00:18:58,890 --> 00:19:02,450
I assure you the Hand wants no part of it.

282
00:19:02,450 --> 00:19:04,640
Call it what you will, Lord Stark, Ser,

283
00:19:04,640 --> 00:19:07,730
the city is packed with people and more flooding in every day.

284
00:19:08,350 --> 00:19:10,880
Last night we had a tavern riot, a brothel fire,

285
00:19:10,920 --> 00:19:13,850
three stabbings and a drunken horse race down the Street of Sisters.

286
00:19:13,850 --> 00:19:16,620
- Dreadful. - If you can't keep the King's peace,

287
00:19:16,620 --> 00:19:19,480
perhaps the City Watch should be commanded by someone who can.

288
00:19:19,480 --> 00:19:21,520
- I need more men. - You'll get 50.

289
00:19:21,550 --> 00:19:24,050
- Lord Baelish will see it paid for. - I will?

290
00:19:24,050 --> 00:19:26,220
You found money for a champion's purse,

291
00:19:26,250 --> 00:19:27,900
you can find money to keep the peace.

292
00:19:30,460 --> 00:19:32,360
I'll also give you 20 of my household guard

293
00:19:32,360 --> 00:19:33,770
till the crowds have left.

294
00:19:33,970 --> 00:19:35,520
Thank you, my Lord Hand, ser.

295
00:19:35,550 --> 00:19:37,340
They will be put to good use.

296
00:19:39,520 --> 00:19:41,490
The sooner this is over, the better.

297
00:19:41,490 --> 00:19:44,550
The realm prospers from such events, my lord.

298
00:19:44,580 --> 00:19:46,260
They give the great a chance at glory,

299
00:19:46,260 --> 00:19:48,610
and the lowly a respite from their woes.

300
00:19:48,610 --> 00:19:51,270
And every inn in the city is full.

301
00:19:51,270 --> 00:19:53,620
And the whores are walking bow-legged.

302
00:19:53,650 --> 00:19:56,150
I'm sure the tourney puts coins in many a pocket.

303
00:19:56,450 --> 00:20:00,670
Now... If there's nothing else, my lords?

304
00:20:10,820 --> 00:20:12,700
Oh, this heat.

305
00:20:12,910 --> 00:20:17,850
On days like this, I envy you northerners your summer snows.

306
00:20:18,250 --> 00:20:19,220
Until tomorrow, my lord...

307
00:20:19,220 --> 00:20:21,710
I've been hoping to talk to you about Jon Arryn.

308
00:20:22,500 --> 00:20:24,150
Lord Arryn?

309
00:20:24,700 --> 00:20:26,650
Oh, his death was

310
00:20:26,680 --> 00:20:28,980
a great sadness to all of us.

311
00:20:29,450 --> 00:20:31,520
I took personal charge of his care,

312
00:20:31,520 --> 00:20:33,890
but I could not save him.

313
00:20:34,470 --> 00:20:36,690
His sickness struck him

314
00:20:36,690 --> 00:20:40,640
very hard and very fast.

315
00:20:41,070 --> 00:20:42,720
I saw him in my chambers

316
00:20:42,720 --> 00:20:44,850
just the night before he passed.

317
00:20:44,850 --> 00:20:48,580
Lord Jon often came to me for counsel.

318
00:20:48,580 --> 00:20:49,970
Why?

319
00:20:51,490 --> 00:20:54,620
I have been grand maester for many years.

320
00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:57,720
Kings and hands have come to me for advice since...

321
00:20:57,750 --> 00:21:00,520
What did Jon want the night before he died?

322
00:21:00,720 --> 00:21:03,280
Oh, he came inquiring after a book.

323
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:04,460
A book?

324
00:21:05,530 --> 00:21:06,580
What book?

325
00:21:06,620 --> 00:21:11,120
Oh, I fear it would be of little interest to you, my lord.

326
00:21:11,150 --> 00:21:13,620
A ponderous tome.

327
00:21:13,650 --> 00:21:17,350
No, I'd like to read it.

328
00:21:19,750 --> 00:21:22,440
"The Lineages and Histories

329
00:21:22,630 --> 00:21:25,830
of the Great Houses of the Seven Kingdoms,

330
00:21:26,020 --> 00:21:29,500
With Descriptions of Many High Lords

331
00:21:29,900 --> 00:21:33,450
and Noble Ladies and Their Children."

332
00:21:51,780 --> 00:21:54,020
"Harkon Umber, first of his name,

333
00:21:54,050 --> 00:21:57,820
born to Lord Hother Umber and Lady Amaryllis Umber

334
00:21:57,850 --> 00:22:00,960
in the 183rd year after Aegon's Landing,

335
00:22:01,210 --> 00:22:03,750
at the Last Hearth. Blue of eye, brown of hair

336
00:22:03,780 --> 00:22:07,320
and fair complected, died in his 14th year

337
00:22:07,350 --> 00:22:09,910
of a wound sustained in a bear hunt."

338
00:22:09,910 --> 00:22:13,380
As I said, my lord, a ponderous read.

339
00:22:13,420 --> 00:22:16,250
Did Jon Arryn tell you what he wanted with it?

340
00:22:16,280 --> 00:22:19,050
He did not, my lord. And I did not presume to ask.

341
00:22:19,080 --> 00:22:21,370
- Jon's death... - Such a tragedy.

342
00:22:21,370 --> 00:22:23,960
...Did he say anything to you during his final hours?

343
00:22:23,960 --> 00:22:25,890
Nothing of import, my lord.

344
00:22:25,890 --> 00:22:28,750
Oh... there was one phrase

345
00:22:28,780 --> 00:22:30,370
He kept repeating:

346
00:22:31,080 --> 00:22:34,120
"The seed is strong," I think it was.

347
00:22:34,150 --> 00:22:35,340
"The seed is strong"?

348
00:22:36,480 --> 00:22:38,780
What does that mean?

349
00:22:38,780 --> 00:22:42,160
The dying mind is a demented mind, Lord Stark.

350
00:22:42,540 --> 00:22:44,450
For all the weight they're given,

351
00:22:44,660 --> 00:22:48,620
last words are usually as significant as first words.

352
00:22:48,650 --> 00:22:51,580
And you're quite certain he died of a natural illness?

353
00:22:51,620 --> 00:22:53,460
What else could it be?

354
00:22:54,670 --> 00:22:56,110
Poison.

355
00:22:56,960 --> 00:22:58,610
A disturbing thought.

356
00:22:58,820 --> 00:23:01,740
No no no, I don't think it likely.

357
00:23:01,740 --> 00:23:04,030
The Hand was loved by all.

358
00:23:04,030 --> 00:23:05,580
What sort of man would dare...

359
00:23:05,580 --> 00:23:08,420
I've heard it said that poison is a woman's weapon.

360
00:23:08,750 --> 00:23:12,620
Yes, women, cravens...

361
00:23:13,220 --> 00:23:15,270
and eunuchs.

362
00:23:16,350 --> 00:23:19,320
Did you know that Lord Varys is a eunuch?

363
00:23:19,350 --> 00:23:20,950
Everybody knows that.

364
00:23:21,130 --> 00:23:23,440
Yes yes, of course.

365
00:23:23,630 --> 00:23:26,550
How that sort of person found himself

366
00:23:26,580 --> 00:23:29,220
on the king's council, I will never know.

367
00:23:29,250 --> 00:23:30,420
I've taken enough of your time.

368
00:23:30,620 --> 00:23:33,380
No trouble at all, my lord. It's a great honor...

369
00:23:33,620 --> 00:23:34,970
Thank you.

370
00:23:35,380 --> 00:23:37,050
I'll find my own way out.

371
00:23:59,850 --> 00:24:01,530
Syrio says a water dancer

372
00:24:01,530 --> 00:24:04,040
can stand on one toe for hours.

373
00:24:04,170 --> 00:24:06,380
It's a hard fall down these steps.

374
00:24:06,380 --> 00:24:08,780
Syrio says every hurt is a lesson

375
00:24:08,780 --> 00:24:10,900
and every lesson makes you better.

376
00:24:12,220 --> 00:24:15,020
- Tomorrow I'm going to be chasing cats. - Cats?

377
00:24:15,650 --> 00:24:16,750
Syrio says.

378
00:24:16,780 --> 00:24:18,850
He says every swordsman should study cats.

379
00:24:18,880 --> 00:24:21,950
They're quiet as shadows and as light as feathers.

380
00:24:21,980 --> 00:24:24,250
You have to be quick to catch them.

381
00:24:24,280 --> 00:24:25,820
He's right about that.

382
00:24:26,150 --> 00:24:27,650
Now that Bran's awake

383
00:24:28,460 --> 00:24:30,170
will he come live with us?

384
00:24:32,150 --> 00:24:35,790
Well, he needs to get his strength back first.

385
00:24:35,790 --> 00:24:38,110
He wants to be a knight of the Kingsguard.

386
00:24:38,340 --> 00:24:42,550
- He can't be one now, can he? - No.

387
00:24:44,120 --> 00:24:45,640
But someday

388
00:24:45,960 --> 00:24:48,040
he could be lord of a holdfast

389
00:24:48,620 --> 00:24:50,570
or sit on the king's council.

390
00:24:51,390 --> 00:24:55,690
Or he might raise castles like Brandon the Builder.

391
00:24:56,380 --> 00:24:58,930
Can I be lord of a holdfast?

392
00:25:03,020 --> 00:25:05,490
You will marry a high lord

393
00:25:05,490 --> 00:25:07,580
and rule his castle.

394
00:25:08,080 --> 00:25:10,380
And your sons shall be knights

395
00:25:10,380 --> 00:25:13,160
and princes and lords.

396
00:25:14,480 --> 00:25:16,760
No.

397
00:25:18,080 --> 00:25:19,810
That's not me.

398
00:25:42,680 --> 00:25:44,270
Hello.

399
00:25:46,320 --> 00:25:48,820
Ser Alliser said I'm to be your new watch partner.

400
00:25:50,900 --> 00:25:52,620
I should warn you,

401
00:25:53,180 --> 00:25:55,210
I don't see all that well.

402
00:25:56,940 --> 00:25:58,950
Come stand by the fire.

403
00:25:59,930 --> 00:26:01,810
- It's warmer. - No, that's all right. I'm fine.

404
00:26:01,810 --> 00:26:04,310
You're not. You're freezing.

405
00:26:16,550 --> 00:26:18,640
I don't like high places.

406
00:26:19,300 --> 00:26:21,250
You can't fight.

407
00:26:21,280 --> 00:26:23,170
You can't see.

408
00:26:23,360 --> 00:26:26,510
You're afraid of heights and almost everything else probably.

409
00:26:28,270 --> 00:26:30,420
What are you doing here, Sam?

410
00:26:34,560 --> 00:26:38,500
On the morning of my 18th nameday, my father came to me.

411
00:26:39,130 --> 00:26:41,830
"You're almost a man now," He said...

412
00:26:42,910 --> 00:26:46,100
"but you're not worthy of my land and title.

413
00:26:47,220 --> 00:26:49,890
Tomorrow, you're going to take the black,

414
00:26:49,890 --> 00:26:53,140
forsake all claim to your inheritance and start north.

415
00:26:54,110 --> 00:26:57,760
If you do not," He said...

416
00:26:58,620 --> 00:27:00,450
"then we'll have a hunt

417
00:27:01,150 --> 00:27:04,110
and somewhere in these woods your horse will stumble

418
00:27:04,260 --> 00:27:07,030
and you'll be thrown from your saddle to die.

419
00:27:09,280 --> 00:27:11,130
Or so I'll tell your mother.

420
00:27:12,180 --> 00:27:14,420
Nothing would please me more."

421
00:27:19,150 --> 00:27:22,210
Ser Alliser's going to make me fight again tomorrow, isn't he?

422
00:27:23,640 --> 00:27:25,570
Yes, he is.

423
00:27:26,530 --> 00:27:28,770
I'm not going to get any better, you know?

424
00:27:29,820 --> 00:27:31,070
Well...

425
00:27:31,790 --> 00:27:33,780
you can't get any worse.

426
00:27:43,470 --> 00:27:45,970
I hear you're reading a boring book.

427
00:27:48,200 --> 00:27:49,920
Pycelle talks too much.

428
00:27:49,920 --> 00:27:51,910
Oh, he never stops.

429
00:27:52,120 --> 00:27:54,400
Do you know Ser Hugh of the Vale?

430
00:27:55,750 --> 00:27:58,150
Not surprising. Until recently,

431
00:27:58,180 --> 00:28:01,870
he was only a squire... Jon Arryn's squire.

432
00:28:01,870 --> 00:28:03,720
He was knighted almost immediately

433
00:28:03,750 --> 00:28:06,130
after his master's untimely death.

434
00:28:06,130 --> 00:28:07,890
Knighted for what?

435
00:28:09,600 --> 00:28:10,780
Why are you telling me this?

436
00:28:10,780 --> 00:28:13,170
I promised Cat that I'd help you.

437
00:28:13,360 --> 00:28:15,030
Where is Ser Hugh?

438
00:28:15,380 --> 00:28:18,040
- I'll speak to him. - A singularly bad idea.

439
00:28:18,040 --> 00:28:20,570
Do you see that boy there?

440
00:28:20,760 --> 00:28:22,960
One of Varys's little birds.

441
00:28:23,180 --> 00:28:26,980
The Spider has taken a great interest in your comings and goings.

442
00:28:27,020 --> 00:28:28,560
Now look there.

443
00:28:28,950 --> 00:28:31,140
That one belongs to the Queen.

444
00:28:32,080 --> 00:28:35,590
And do you see that septa pretending to read her book?

445
00:28:35,990 --> 00:28:37,750
Varys or the Queen?

446
00:28:37,780 --> 00:28:40,460
No, she's one of mine.

447
00:28:42,330 --> 00:28:45,600
Is there someone in your service whom you trust completely?

448
00:28:47,520 --> 00:28:51,430
- Yes. - The wiser answer was no, my lord.

449
00:28:52,110 --> 00:28:54,980
Get a message to this paragon of yours...

450
00:28:55,020 --> 00:28:58,540
discreetly... send him to question Ser Hugh.

451
00:28:58,920 --> 00:28:59,860
After that, you might want him

452
00:28:59,860 --> 00:29:02,840
to visit a certain armorer in the city.

453
00:29:03,020 --> 00:29:06,080
He lives in a large house at the top of the Street of Steel.

454
00:29:06,120 --> 00:29:08,770
- Why? - I have my observers, as I said,

455
00:29:08,770 --> 00:29:11,050
and it's possible that they saw Lord Arryn

456
00:29:11,080 --> 00:29:15,110
visit this armorer several times in the weeks before his death.

457
00:29:16,580 --> 00:29:19,880
Lord Baelish, perhaps I was wrong to distrust you.

458
00:29:19,880 --> 00:29:21,550
Distrusting me was the wisest thing

459
00:29:21,580 --> 00:29:24,170
you've done since you climbed off your horse.

460
00:29:31,820 --> 00:29:33,780
... Two, three,

461
00:29:33,820 --> 00:29:35,620
24, five,

462
00:29:35,650 --> 00:29:37,950
six, 27,

463
00:29:37,980 --> 00:29:39,450
- 28, 29, - Ser Hugh?

464
00:29:39,450 --> 00:29:41,680
30, 31, 32

465
00:29:41,720 --> 00:29:44,280
- Ser Hugh! - As you can see, I'm busy.

466
00:29:44,280 --> 00:29:46,950
I'm here on behalf of Lord Eddard Stark,

467
00:29:46,980 --> 00:29:48,660
the Hand of the King.

468
00:29:49,280 --> 00:29:50,680
I'm the captain of his guard.

469
00:29:50,720 --> 00:29:52,860
I'm sorry. I didn't catch your name, ser...

470
00:29:53,060 --> 00:29:55,200
No "Ser." I'm not a knight.

471
00:29:55,400 --> 00:29:58,970
I see. Well, it just so happens that I am.

472
00:30:04,200 --> 00:30:06,740
He said he'd be glad to talk to the Hand himself.

473
00:30:06,940 --> 00:30:08,220
He's a knight, you see.

474
00:30:08,220 --> 00:30:10,070
Ah, a knight.

475
00:30:10,740 --> 00:30:13,430
They strut around like roosters down here.

476
00:30:14,050 --> 00:30:16,540
Even the ones who've never seen an arrow coming their way.

477
00:30:16,540 --> 00:30:18,350
You shouldn't be out here, my lord.

478
00:30:18,740 --> 00:30:22,060
- There's no telling who has eyes where. - Let them look.

479
00:30:24,140 --> 00:30:27,360
The former Hand did call on me, my lord, several times.

480
00:30:27,740 --> 00:30:30,750
I regret to say he did not honor me with his patronage.

481
00:30:30,770 --> 00:30:32,440
What did Lord Arryn want?

482
00:30:33,130 --> 00:30:35,050
He always came to see the boy.

483
00:30:35,050 --> 00:30:36,720
I'd like to see him as well.

484
00:30:37,030 --> 00:30:38,520
As you wish, my lord.

485
00:30:39,490 --> 00:30:40,800
Gendry!

486
00:30:45,130 --> 00:30:46,410
Here he is.

487
00:30:46,490 --> 00:30:49,450
Strong for his age. He works hard.

488
00:30:50,460 --> 00:30:52,880
Show the hand the helmet you made, lad.

489
00:31:06,650 --> 00:31:09,220
- This is fine work. - It's not for sale.

490
00:31:09,220 --> 00:31:11,220
Boy, this is the King's Hand!

491
00:31:11,220 --> 00:31:14,050
- If his lordship wants the helm... - I made it for me.

492
00:31:14,680 --> 00:31:17,290
- Forgive him, my lord. - There's nothing to forgive.

493
00:31:18,550 --> 00:31:21,810
When Lord Arryn came to visit you, what would you talk about?

494
00:31:22,430 --> 00:31:24,490
He just asked me questions is all, my lord.

495
00:31:24,490 --> 00:31:26,120
What kind of questions?

496
00:31:28,590 --> 00:31:30,840
About my work at first,

497
00:31:31,550 --> 00:31:34,510
if I was being treated well, if I liked it here.

498
00:31:36,640 --> 00:31:38,690
But then he started asking me about my mother.

499
00:31:39,480 --> 00:31:43,050
- Your mother? - Who she was, what she looked like.

500
00:31:43,670 --> 00:31:45,260
What did you tell him?

501
00:31:46,890 --> 00:31:48,740
She died when I was little.

502
00:31:49,810 --> 00:31:51,500
She had yellow hair.

503
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:54,300
She'd sing to me sometimes.

504
00:31:58,230 --> 00:31:59,620
Look at me.

505
00:32:10,120 --> 00:32:11,670
Get back to work, lad.

506
00:32:13,920 --> 00:32:15,180
If the day ever comes when that boy

507
00:32:15,180 --> 00:32:17,450
would rather wield a sword than forge one,

508
00:32:18,770 --> 00:32:20,170
you send him to me.

509
00:32:24,690 --> 00:32:25,970
Find anything?

510
00:32:27,870 --> 00:32:29,900
King Robert's bastard son.

511
00:32:33,960 --> 00:32:35,240
He likes that.

512
00:32:37,290 --> 00:32:40,470
This is for the King from Lord Stark.

513
00:32:41,580 --> 00:32:42,920
- Should I leave it with... - Shhh.

514
00:32:44,180 --> 00:32:45,400
Listen.

515
00:32:46,750 --> 00:32:48,100
Do you hear them?

516
00:32:50,990 --> 00:32:53,380
How many do you think are in there with him?

517
00:32:55,410 --> 00:32:57,780
- Guess. - Three?

518
00:32:58,270 --> 00:32:59,490
Four.

519
00:33:00,970 --> 00:33:03,340
He likes to do this when I'm on duty...

520
00:33:03,680 --> 00:33:06,540
he makes me listen as he insults my sister.

521
00:33:11,610 --> 00:33:13,690
- Forgive me, my lord... - Why do I have to forgive you?

522
00:33:13,690 --> 00:33:15,190
Have you wronged me?

523
00:33:16,950 --> 00:33:18,720
We've met before, you know.

524
00:33:18,720 --> 00:33:21,720
Have we? Strange, I've forgotten.

525
00:33:21,930 --> 00:33:23,180
The siege of Pyke,

526
00:33:23,180 --> 00:33:25,260
we fought side by side one afternoon.

527
00:33:27,220 --> 00:33:29,270
- That's where you got your scar? - Aye.

528
00:33:30,800 --> 00:33:32,780
One of the Greyjoys nearly took my eye.

529
00:33:32,780 --> 00:33:35,390
- Vicious sons of whores. - They like their bloodshed.

530
00:33:35,390 --> 00:33:37,450
They stopped liking it at the end.

531
00:33:38,710 --> 00:33:41,820
